WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our next guest
is an Oscar-nominated actress

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.770
you know from films
like "The Lost Daughter"

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.173
and "Wild Rose," as well
as the HBO series "Chernobyl."

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.242
She stars in "Women Talking,"
which is in select theaters now

00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:13.146
and everywhere January 20th.
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:14.714
-Let's move on
to the cons of leading.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:18.851
-We the women will decide
what happens in these meetings.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:21.554
I'm not a two-bit failed farmer
who must teach.

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.289
You have been invited here.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.557
You have been invited here

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.626
to listen to what we have to say
and write it down.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:30.696
Nothing more. Just listen.

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:33.900
-Please welcome to the show
Jessie Buckley, everyone.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.668
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:41.841 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:48.014 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.449
Jessie, I'm so happy
you're here. How are you?

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.918
-I'm great.
-I'm glad to hear it.

00:00:52.918 --> 00:00:56.556
This is a wonderful film.
It's a very dark subject matter.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.859
It's a true story
based on a Mennonite community

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.362
where this group of women
had to decide

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.963
after these acts
of sexual violence,

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.597
are we going to
stay in the community

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:06.999
or are we going to leave?

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:09.935
It's very specific, but
the story feels very universal.

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:12.772
Was that something you felt
you were all trying to do

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.308
when you were sitting
in that barn together?

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.878
-Yeah, I mean we all
wore socks and sandals

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:24.550
and had our legs really hairy
and kind of got into it.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.754
But, you know, there's a lot of
things to be a bit angry about

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.889
at the moment as women.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:34.394
You know, girls?

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.862
Yeah, so I guess there is --
And it was specific,

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:39.832
but I think the thing that Sarah
did so brilliantly about it

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.201
was to make it universal

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:43.002
and to make it
a universal conversation.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.871
And, yeah, it was brilliant.
-You mentioned Sarah.

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:48.274
That's Sarah Polley,
the incredible director
of this film.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.076
And I've read a lot
of interviews with her

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.978
and those of you in the cast.

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:55.080
And even though it was this dark
subject matter, it did seem --

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.550
and I was happy to read it --
that you all also had

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.418
a great deal of fun
making the movie,

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.087
which is the way it should be.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.689
-[Laughing]
Oh, we had so much fun.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.192
And actually, I was, like,
laughing at that clip of Ben.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.962
Poor Ben Whishaw. I gave him
an awful time on this.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.830
And we're, like
really good friends,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.366
and he would be my Friday wife,
which basically meant

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:16.869
that he went and drank wine
with me every Friday.

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:18.904
Everybody needs a Friday wife.

00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:22.007
I'm up to being somebody's
Friday wife this Friday

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:24.443
if anybody -- if anybody wants.

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.779
-I think there will be
a lot of takers.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.414
-So we had lots of fun,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:31.017
and, yeah, Rooney brought
a fart machine onto set.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.786
-See, that's very -- Rooney Mara
is wonderful in this film.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.122
And watching the film,
it's surprising to me

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.291
that she would ever have had
a fart machine on set.

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:40.026
-It's always the quiet ones.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.293
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.293 --> 00:02:42.828
-That is true.
-Silent but violent.

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.597
-Absolutely.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:48.568
-So it's filmed in a very rural
area in this beautiful barn.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:52.972
You actually -- You just sort of
left London for the sort of --

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:56.242
the wilderness, I guess,
a rural area you bought.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.511
-Oh, right, yeah. I didn't know
what you were going to say.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.612
Yeah, I did.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.714
-I'd make a terrible
Friday wife.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.482
-I don't think so.
-You'd just sit drinking wine

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:06.919
being like,
"What is he talking about?"

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:08.821
-Yeah, I moved out of London,

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:12.859
and I moved
to a very, very old house

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:16.362
that has these mad witch
markings over the fireplace,

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:20.200
and it's falling down, and you
kind of, like, put it back up

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.001
with a bit of Pritt stick
or something.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.836
Just stay, stay!
-Were they surprised

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:26.005
you wanted to buy it
when they sold it to you?

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:30.109
Because you're not describing
what sounds look a gem.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.411
-It is a gem. It's amazing.

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:34.013
The people
that we bought it from,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.882
he'd lived in the village
his whole life,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.916
and he just fell in love
with this house,

00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:40.419
and he put all his last pennies
towards buying this.

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:43.790
It would have been like
an old farmer house,

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.694
you know, people who worked
on the local farm, obviously.

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:49.428
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:53.032
And he restored it exactly to
what it was like in the 1500s.

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:55.501
So all these amazing kind of --

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.003
All the walls
are the original paint,

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.972
like pig's blood and lime wash
and all the old floors.

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:01.540
And it's so cool.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.176
It's so cool to be in a house
that's way older than you.

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:07.513
-Yeah, it is.
That sounds very -- You --

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.015
When you were growing up,
your father owned a guesthouse,

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:12.818
so this is a hotel of sorts,
like an inn?

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:15.020
-Yeah, like --
[ Laughs ]

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.923
"Argh! You..."

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:21.060
-You would sort of hold the
lantern and talk like a pirate?

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.896
-"Come in, come in!"

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.132
-Did you have --
This is in Ireland.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:29.702
What were your -- Were your
guests other Irish people?

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.470
Were they Americans?
Who would come?

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.106
-We actually -- Because I lived
in a town called Killarney,

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:36.642
which is a really beautiful,
kind of, lake district,

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:39.578
lots of mountains, and
a lot of Americans would come.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:43.282
And it was so exciting.
"The Americans are coming."

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:45.151
[ Laughs ]

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.620
And we'd, like --
We all had to help, you know,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.655
because that was
the family business,

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:51.357
and we'd bring them out
breakfast.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.325
And then if they were having
dinner in the evening,

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:54.993
we'd all do a little show
for them.

00:04:54.993 --> 00:04:57.429
So there's these really
embarrassing pictures of me

00:04:57.429 --> 00:05:00.466
furiously Irish dancing,
which I was crap at.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.101
I was so bad at it.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.404
I'm like way too, kind of,
all over the place

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:05.905
to keep my hands by my side.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.674
But we had a good time, yeah.

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:10.809
-Do you think that
most Americans can't tell

00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:12.745
when someone's crap
at Irish dancing?

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:14.180
Do you think that
you got away with it?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.282
-I think we got away with it.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:18.684
I also heard
you were in a production

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.686
of "West Side Story" as a kid.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:21.954
What --
Can you just talk us through --

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.891
What does an all-Irish
"West Side Story"...?

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.224
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.224 --> 00:05:28.127
Like, how did that --

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.430
Do you try to effect
an American accent,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.534
or is it just
Irish Sharks and Irish Jets?

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.201
-Irish dance. Irish dancing.

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:38.771
The Jets are like --
The Jets are called Jesus.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.274
[ Laughs ]

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:44.443
Well, I went to, like,
an all-girls kind of

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:47.246
Catholic school, so to divide
the girls from the girls,

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:50.516
one of them -- I played Tony
in "West Side Story."

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.118
So I was actually was wearing

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:55.020
a big red suit
pretty much similar to this.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.423
And they would kind of
give us French plaits,

00:05:57.423 --> 00:06:00.192
and for some reason, they
used to, like, draw red dots

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:02.428
in the kind of corner
of our eyes because they said

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.463
that the audience would think
we were cross-eyed out there

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:07.533
if we didn't have
these red dots.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.868
-Wait. How would that work?

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.570
-I have no -- I don't know.
I don't know.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:14.273
I felt like, wow, yeah, I must
put these red dots in the corner

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.341
of my eye if
I'm going to be an actress.

00:06:16.341 --> 00:06:18.377
[ Laughs ]

00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:20.679
-Now I'm worried. I've never
done that on this show.

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:22.782
Everybody at home must think
I'm cross-eyed.

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:24.116
-Yeah.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:27.487
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]
Congratulations on the film.

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:29.088
It's really wonderful
and beautiful,

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.856
and it's so nice
having you here, Jessie.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.724
-Aww. Thanks for having me.
-I'm a big fan.

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:34.093
Jessie Buckley, everybody.

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.362
"Women Talking"
is in select theaters now

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:37.730
and everywhere January 20th.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.732 line:0%
We'll be right back
with Stephen Markley.

